Add the device tree node the WLAN host interface binds to.

Wi-Fi stage 2b. The ported MT6625L driver registers its own top-level platform
device, so it needs nothing from the device tree for that - but its AHB host
interface is a separate platform driver that binds by compatible:

	struct platform_driver MtkPltmAhbDriver = {
		.driver = { .name = "mt-wifi",
			    .of_match_table = apwifi_of_ids },
		.probe = HifAhbPltmProbe,
	};

	static const struct of_device_id apwifi_of_ids[] = {
		{.compatible = "mediatek,wifi",},
	};

and then looks the same node up again by compatible, twice, in glBusSetIrq()
and glBusFreeIrq(), to find the interrupt to hand to request_irq(). So the node
has to exist even though nothing references it by phandle.

Note that the driver reads all three interrupt cells with
of_property_read_u32_array() and uses the third as the flags for request_irq(),
rather than letting irq_of_parse_and_map() carry the trigger type. The trigger
type here is therefore load-bearing, not decorative.

Values are the vendor's own, from mt8163.dtsi in the 3.18 tree. Checked against
this tree before adding: 0x180f0000 is unclaimed, GIC_SPI 238 is unused, and
CLK_INFRA_AP_DMA exists (infracfg ICG1 bit 18).

The node also carries mediatek,consys, pointing at the same CONSYS the
Bluetooth node uses. The WLAN power amplifier LDO is not a separate rail - the
vendor's mtk_wcn_consys_hw_wifi_paldo_ctrl() is a one-line wrapper around its
bt_paldo_ctrl() - so our glue reaches mt8163_consys_bt_paldo_set() through this
phandle.

*** Placement matters, and the first version of this patch got it wrong. ***

It anchored on consys_mem: consys-pool, on the assumption that it sat beside
consys@18070000. It does not: consys-pool lives inside &{/reserved-memory}, so
the node was created at /reserved-memory/wifi@180f0000. of_platform_populate()
does not walk reserved-memory, so no platform device was ever created and the
AHB driver could not bind - the node was present in /proc/device-tree and
completely inert. Confirmed on hardware before correcting it: the node appeared
under /proc/device-tree/reserved-memory/ and nothing matching appeared in
/sys/bus/platform/devices.

It now goes inside &{/soc}, next to consys@18070000, which is where a SoC
peripheral belongs and which does get populated.

diff -urN a/arch/arm64/boot/dts/mediatek/mt8163-amazon-biscuit.dts b/arch/arm64/boot/dts/mediatek/mt8163-amazon-biscuit.dts
--- a/arch/arm64/boot/dts/mediatek/mt8163-amazon-biscuit.dts	2026-08-14 00:39:13.943668838 +0000
+++ b/arch/arm64/boot/dts/mediatek/mt8163-amazon-biscuit.dts	2026-08-14 00:39:14.002655643 +0000
@@ -748,6 +748,38 @@
 	 * the bus-protection handshake), the connectivity processor reset,
 	 * and the three PMIC rails the vendor tree brings up alongside them.
 	 */
+	/*
+	 * The WLAN side of the same CONSYS block. The ported MT6625L driver's
+	 * AHB host interface binds to this by compatible, and looks it up
+	 * again by compatible in glBusSetIrq() and glBusFreeIrq() to find its
+	 * interrupt, so the node must exist even though nothing references it
+	 * by phandle.
+	 *
+	 * The driver reads all three interrupt cells with
+	 * of_property_read_u32_array() and passes the third to request_irq()
+	 * as the flags, rather than letting irq_of_parse_and_map() carry the
+	 * trigger type - so the trigger here is load-bearing.
+	 *
+	 * Values are the vendor's own, from mt8163.dtsi in the 3.18 tree.
+	 * Checked against this tree: 0x180f0000 is unclaimed, GIC_SPI 238 is
+	 * unused, and CLK_INFRA_AP_DMA exists.
+	 */
+	wifi: wifi@180f0000 {
+		compatible = "mediatek,wifi";
+		reg = <0 0x180f0000 0 0x005c>;
+		interrupts = <GIC_SPI 238 IRQ_TYPE_LEVEL_LOW>;
+		clocks = <&infracfg CLK_INFRA_AP_DMA>;
+		clock-names = "wifi-dma";
+		/*
+		 * The same CONSYS the Bluetooth node points at. The WLAN power
+		 * amplifier LDO is not a separate rail - the vendor's
+		 * wifi_paldo_ctrl entry point simply calls its bt_paldo_ctrl
+		 * one - so the driver reaches mt8163_consys_bt_paldo_set()
+		 * through this phandle.
+		 */
+		mediatek,consys = <&consys>;
+	};
+
 	consys: consys@18070000 {
 		compatible = "mediatek,mt8163-consys";
 		reg = <0 0x18070000 0 0x200>;
